{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"**CONFIRMED DEFECT IN THIS TICKET'S OWN IMPLEMENTATION — DO NOT CLOSE 3768. The change is a NO-OP at runtime for the code-review gate, and my tests did not catch it.**\n\nEVIDENCE, from a real gate run with per-call logging (`REBAR_USAGE_LOG`, which records one JSON object per LLM call at the runner chokepoint — `runner.py:545`):\n    rebar -c verify.enable_code_review=true review-code --base HEAD~2 --head HEAD~1\n    REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE=<inverted>.toml   REBAR_USAGE_LOG=<jsonl>\nConfig (INVERTED on purpose): frontier=`bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8`, standard=`anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6`.\nSix per-call records, ALL on `anthropic:claude-opus-4-8` (provider=anthropic):\n    code-review-base                            in=787,803\n    code-review-docs                            in= 22,052\n    code-review-tests                           in=699,425\n    code-review-deletion-impact                 in= 98,838\n    code-review-project-review-phase-boundaries in= 22,168\n    code-review-api-compat                      in=330,696\n`base` declares `model: frontier`, which under this config MUST resolve to `bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8`. It resolved to the bare default instead. So the declaration is not reaching the resolver.\n\n**BOTH COMPETING EXPLANATIONS WERE TESTED — this is not an inference from one observation.**\n* H2 \"the config pointer was not loaded, so `frontier` fell back to its BUILT-IN default (also claude-opus-4-8)\" would produce the IDENTICAL log. ELIMINATED: with the same env var, `load_class_slots()` -> `resolve_class('frontier')` returns `bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8`, both with and without `repo_root=` (the form the gate uses).\n* H1 \"the step's `model:` never reaches `resolve_model`\" is therefore what remains. Corroborated: `resolve_model(cfg, step='frontier')` -> `bedrock:...` but `resolve_model(cfg, step=None)` -> `claude-opus-4-8`, which is exactly what was observed.\nALSO RULED OUT: the running package loads MY worktree gate — `rebar/llm/workflow/gates/code-review.yaml` under rebar-wt-provider-seam, with all five declarations intact — and `code_review/shim.py:119 review_code` really does execute the gate via `gate_dispatch.produce_code_review_verdict`.\n\n**WHY MY 9 TESTS PASSED ANYWAY — the honest post-mortem.** They assert two things: (1) the YAML carries the right class token in the right place, and (2) `resolve_model_string(<that token>)` resolves correctly under a Bedrock config. Neither asserts THE ENGINE PASSES THE TOKEN TO THE RESOLVER DURING A RUN. That is precisely the gap this plan's own text warns about — \"assert the RESOLVED model reaching each step at RUNTIME, never merely that the YAML carries a class token\" — and I did not actually do it. The 6/6 mutation results are consistent with that: every mutation I ran perturbed the YAML or the class table, so every one was caught by a YAML/resolution assertion. No mutation could distinguish \"declaration honoured\" from \"declaration ignored\", because no test observes the runtime binding.\n\nNEXT STEPS (do not close until these are done):\n1. Find where the code-review gate's step model is (not) plumbed. `CodeReviewBatchRunner` (`code_review/batch_runner.py:60-61,83-84`) DOES resolve `req.model_ladder[0]` and set `step['model']`, so the batch path looks correct in isolation — determine whether `interpreter.py:388` is even the constructor used for this gate, and how `base` (a single-shot prompt step) reaches `runs.py:154`'s `resolve_model(cfg, step=ctx.step.get('model'), ...)`.\n2. Add an END-TO-END test that would have caught this: execute the gate with a RECORDING runner and assert the model each step receives. Deterministic, free, and strictly stronger than the live probe.\n3. Only then re-run the live probe as the [operator-attested] evidence.\n\nThe Gerrit change 1169 is +1/+1 and submittable, but it should NOT be treated as delivering the behaviour until this is fixed — it currently ships YAML that the runtime ignores."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785606387248076001,"uuid":"37d110f0-fdc2-4397-841f-9896b8d1ccac"}